WEBVTT

00:00.240 --> 00:01.440
Instructor: In this section, we cover

00:01.440 --> 00:04.402
one of the most useful features in Git, branching.

00:04.402 --> 00:07.208
So everything in this section has to do with branching.

00:07.208 --> 00:10.800
And branching is really one of the last topics, I would say,

00:10.800 --> 00:13.920
that you have to master and have to be comfortable with

00:13.920 --> 00:16.410
in order to use Git day-to-day.

00:16.410 --> 00:18.872
There are plenty of other topics we cover in this course

00:18.872 --> 00:21.900
but those may not come up as frequently.

00:21.900 --> 00:23.850
Branching will come up on a daily basis,

00:23.850 --> 00:27.210
just like committing, if you work with Git daily at least.

00:27.210 --> 00:30.090
So we start by covering branching at a conceptual level.

00:30.090 --> 00:31.005
What is it?

00:31.005 --> 00:32.310
What are the benefits?

00:32.310 --> 00:33.600
Why would you use it?

00:33.600 --> 00:35.392
And that is going to be critical.

00:35.392 --> 00:37.659
I've marked it in yellow, not because it's hard,

00:37.659 --> 00:39.990
but it does take up a little bit more time

00:39.990 --> 00:41.550
than learning a single command.

00:41.550 --> 00:43.410
But I really recommend you don't skip

00:43.410 --> 00:45.750
through the videos before the commands.

00:45.750 --> 00:48.150
Everyone likes to just get to the meaty stuff,

00:48.150 --> 00:49.320
show me the commands.

00:49.320 --> 00:50.850
But with branching it really helps

00:50.850 --> 00:52.302
to understand with diagrams.

00:52.302 --> 00:54.960
Plus I introduced a couple of concepts

00:54.960 --> 00:56.940
including something called Git HEAD.

00:56.940 --> 00:59.121
You've probably seen that term so far in your terminal.

00:59.121 --> 01:01.410
That's also going to be critical,

01:01.410 --> 01:05.160
understanding what HEAD is and the fact that it points

01:05.160 --> 01:06.480
to something called a branch reference

01:06.480 --> 01:08.310
and a branch reference points to a commit.

01:08.310 --> 01:11.610
All this stuff we cover in these two videos.

01:11.610 --> 01:14.490
Other crucial things, understanding the Git branch command,

01:14.490 --> 01:17.295
the Git switch command, the Git checkout command.

01:17.295 --> 01:19.530
Then I'll say that deleting

01:19.530 --> 01:21.431
and renaming branches is important

01:21.431 --> 01:24.630
but not as important as this other stuff.

01:24.630 --> 01:27.150
HEAD and refs behind the scenes

01:27.150 --> 01:28.620
I'm gonna say is nice to have.

01:28.620 --> 01:30.660
This is just an explanation of,

01:30.660 --> 01:33.600
or it's proof of how some of these concepts work.

01:33.600 --> 01:36.270
I actually show you in the .Git directory.

01:36.270 --> 01:39.060
So it's nice to have, it'll make you a more, I guess,

01:39.060 --> 01:41.370
well-rounded or informed Git developer

01:41.370 --> 01:43.680
but you don't need to know it in order to use Git.

01:43.680 --> 01:45.750
And then we spend a little bit of time,

01:45.750 --> 01:48.627
not a whole lot, talking about branch naming

01:48.627 --> 01:52.901
and the issue that came up in late 2020, early 2021

01:52.901 --> 01:57.330
around renaming the default branch from Master to Main.

01:57.330 --> 02:00.110
So that will make more sense once we talk about branches.

02:00.110 --> 02:02.220
But I'm gonna say it's important

02:02.220 --> 02:05.047
but it's not critical to understand in order to use Git.
